<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mount Lebanon</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mount <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> is a mountain range running parallel to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast of Lebanon, and the name of the historic region in its highlands. Its valleys gave refuge to many communities, notably the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maronites">Maronite Christians</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a>. In antiquity the Ituraeans held parts of it, and in 1861 it became an autonomous Ottoman district, the core of the later Lebanese state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mount-lebanon.jpg" alt="Mount Lebanon" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Linaduliban at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bsharridistrictmountains.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
